      "text": "We are given the equation:\n\n\\[ x + y = 4 \\]\n\\[ 3xy = 4 \\]\n\nFirst, we solve the quadratic equation that results from the symmetric sums of the roots. Let \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) be the roots of the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ t^2 - (x+y)t + xy = 0 \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values \\( x + y = 4 \\) and \\( 3xy = 4 \\), we get:\n\n\\[ t^2 - 4t + \\frac{4}{3} = 0 \\]\n\nTo clear the fraction, multiply through by 3:\n\n\\[ 3t^2 - 12t + 4 = 0 \\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 3 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 4 \\):\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}}{2 \\cdot 3} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 48}}{6} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{96}}{6} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm 4\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\]\n\\[ t = 2 \\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\]\n\nThus, the solutions \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are:\n\n\\[ x = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}, \\quad y = 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\]\n\nor\n\n\\[ x = 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}, \\quad y = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\]\n\nIn both cases, the form \\( x = \\frac{a \\pm b\\sqrt{c}}{d} \\) matches where \\( a = 6 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 6 \\), and \\( d = 3 \\).\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n\\[ a = 6, \\quad b = 2, \\quad c = 6, \\quad d = 3 \\]\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\n\\[ a + b + c + d = 6 + 2 + 6 + 3 = 17 \\]\n\nThus, the value is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{17} \\]",
